// A private candidate joining a public overload name.
//
// Parse-stage: an overload set is built as each declaration is parsed, so the
// rejection is reported there, on the candidate. A concrete name beginning with
// '_' is reachable only inside its owner, and a public overload name would make
// it reachable from anywhere the name is: an importer selecting it through the
// name would be calling a function it may not see, and the symbol would have
// no sound linkage -- private, yet needed from outside. So the two must agree,
// both private or both public. The refused candidate is left out of the set;
// the set itself, and its other candidates, stand.
//
// The rule is the same for a method; struct-parse carries that case.

// A public candidate on a public name is the ordinary case
fn scaleInt overload scale(n i64) i64 {
  n * 2i64;
}

// A private candidate on the same public name is refused
fn _scaleFloat overload scale(n f64) f64 {  //~ ErrorPrivOverload:1 "_scaleFloat is private, so it may not join the public overload name scale"
  n * 2.0d;
}

// A private name with private candidates is legal; core-overload selects
// through one
fn _hideInt overload _hide(n i64) i64 {
  n;
}

fn _hideFloat overload _hide(n f64) f64 {
  n;
}

// The rule is about the candidate's own name, so it applies to the first
// candidate too, before the set has anything else in it
fn _lonely overload alone(n i32) i32 {  //~ ErrorPrivOverload:1 "_lonely is private, so it may not join the public overload name alone"
  n;
}
